WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.511 --> 00:00:03.376
- He's a Scottish born
chef and restaurateur.

00:00:03.376 --> 00:00:06.484
His restaurants have been
awarded 60 Michelin stars

00:00:06.484 --> 00:00:08.719
and currently hold 14.

00:00:08.719 --> 00:00:10.591
He's known for creating TV programs

00:00:10.591 --> 00:00:13.192
about competitive cookery and food.

00:00:13.192 --> 00:00:14.370
He's Gordon Ramsey,

00:00:14.370 --> 00:00:17.174
and here are his Top 10 rules for success.

00:00:17.174 --> 00:00:20.100
(loud swooshing)

00:00:20.100 --> 00:00:22.680
- My advice to young
chefs from the age of 16

00:00:22.680 --> 00:00:26.263
to sort of 29, 30, is
14 years of a sponge.

00:00:27.251 --> 00:00:28.511
You're absorbing knowledge.

00:00:28.511 --> 00:00:30.299
Don't take a job for the sake of money.

00:00:30.299 --> 00:00:33.103
Don't worry about earning
500 pound a month or year

00:00:33.103 --> 00:00:34.789
or more somewhere else.

00:00:34.789 --> 00:00:36.862
Go and get knowledge, because that becomes

00:00:36.862 --> 00:00:39.097
a bigger passport for everything.

00:00:39.097 --> 00:00:41.311
The money will come once
you've mastered your craft

00:00:41.311 --> 00:00:43.119
and become incredibly talented.

00:00:43.119 --> 00:00:47.023
Work for big chefs and
find a different level

00:00:47.023 --> 00:00:48.608
of comfort.

00:00:48.608 --> 00:00:49.989
When things get too comfortable

00:00:49.989 --> 00:00:51.655
and you're still living with your parents

00:00:51.655 --> 00:00:52.915
and you still got your first job

00:00:52.915 --> 00:00:53.910
and you don't want to move out

00:00:53.910 --> 00:00:55.739
because everything's too
comfortable, get out.

00:00:55.739 --> 00:00:56.697
Put yourself in a strange situation

00:00:56.697 --> 00:00:58.322
in the middle of Barcelona.

00:00:58.322 --> 00:00:59.480
Put yourself in the middle of Paris,

00:00:59.480 --> 00:01:00.557
put yourself in the middle of Belgium

00:01:00.557 --> 00:01:02.324
and see what's available.

00:01:02.324 --> 00:01:04.051
That's amazing how much
confidence it gives you.

00:01:04.051 --> 00:01:06.327
More importantly, it's great to sort of

00:01:06.327 --> 00:01:08.785
eat and travel at the
same time, fantastic.

00:01:08.785 --> 00:01:11.427
If there's one thing I've
learned and experienced,

00:01:11.427 --> 00:01:15.980
is that, if you don't open
up and you don't delegate,

00:01:15.980 --> 00:01:17.890
then you'll kill yourself.

00:01:17.890 --> 00:01:19.841
And if there's one thing
I've managed to understand

00:01:19.841 --> 00:01:21.811
is the art of delegation, because,

00:01:21.811 --> 00:01:24.656
if I can pass on to
Claire Smith and to Arya

00:01:24.656 --> 00:01:27.500
my palate, my understanding,
and my knowledge,

00:01:27.500 --> 00:01:30.263
then grab it, run it, and
then pass it on to your team.

00:01:30.263 --> 00:01:32.542
Don't hold that inside.

00:01:32.542 --> 00:01:34.126
The key to a successful business

00:01:34.126 --> 00:01:36.463
is remembering that the customer is king,

00:01:36.463 --> 00:01:39.328
because without them, we're nobody.

00:01:39.328 --> 00:01:42.374
And so I, from a chef's point of view,

00:01:42.374 --> 00:01:44.412
always put myself in
the customer's situation

00:01:44.412 --> 00:01:46.342
where I see it from their eyes,

00:01:46.342 --> 00:01:47.483
I don't cook for chefs.

00:01:47.483 --> 00:01:49.575
Every time chefs come
into the dining room,

00:01:49.575 --> 00:01:51.770
first thing they do is turn
up the plate upside down,

00:01:51.770 --> 00:01:52.948
and start photographing the food,

00:01:52.948 --> 00:01:56.199
and they're constantly dissecting the food

00:01:56.199 --> 00:01:58.545
as opposed to enjoying it.

00:01:58.545 --> 00:02:01.758
So, I always look at it from
a customer's perspective,

00:02:01.758 --> 00:02:05.882
because they're king, and
without them, we're history.

00:02:05.882 --> 00:02:07.832
That takes years, to become a great chef.

00:02:07.832 --> 00:02:12.323
What you need to do is establish
confidence in yourself.

00:02:12.323 --> 00:02:15.045
Cooking is an amazing journey,

00:02:15.045 --> 00:02:17.585
and don't think you can learn
it within three or four years.

00:02:17.585 --> 00:02:18.926
It's like studying medicine,

00:02:18.926 --> 00:02:20.876
it's over a 10 to 15 year period.

00:02:20.876 --> 00:02:22.644
I'm 42 years of age and I'm still learning

00:02:22.644 --> 00:02:24.283
new and exiting things
now that's I'm bringing

00:02:24.283 --> 00:02:25.432
back to the fold.

00:02:25.432 --> 00:02:27.444
But more importantly, vision, I think.

00:02:27.444 --> 00:02:29.684
I didn't think French was
important, that skill,

00:02:29.684 --> 00:02:32.345
and I kick myself now,
because I would live in France

00:02:32.345 --> 00:02:34.522
for three years, and became bilingual.

00:02:34.522 --> 00:02:35.741
But I wish I'd studied harder

00:02:35.741 --> 00:02:37.631
with a second language under my belt.

00:02:37.631 --> 00:02:38.931
That gives you a different culture,

00:02:38.931 --> 00:02:41.653
gives you a completely
different level of confidence

00:02:41.653 --> 00:02:45.331
learning French, French
cuisine, mannerism,

00:02:45.331 --> 00:02:49.151
and cooking in a very
robust, tenacious way.

00:02:49.151 --> 00:02:52.234
So, yeah, vision and open mindedness.

00:02:53.194 --> 00:02:55.144
And we never cut corners.

00:02:55.144 --> 00:02:57.156
The minute you start
cutting corners in food,

00:02:57.156 --> 00:02:59.898
time out, there's no worse job
to have than cutting corners

00:02:59.898 --> 00:03:00.752
in food.

00:03:00.752 --> 00:03:03.332
So, attention to detail, an
amazing exciting journey,

00:03:03.332 --> 00:03:06.208
one of the very few jobs
anywhere in the world

00:03:06.208 --> 00:03:09.288
where you can travel,
get paid, and experience

00:03:09.288 --> 00:03:12.071
phenomenal food anywhere
in the world, brilliant.

00:03:12.071 --> 00:03:13.615
I'm going to teach them
the common sense rules

00:03:13.615 --> 00:03:16.318
to ensure front of house runs smoothly,

00:03:16.318 --> 00:03:18.329
starting with the basics.

00:03:18.329 --> 00:03:19.957
- On the menu tonight,

00:03:19.957 --> 00:03:23.411
I think everything
should be quit tempting,

00:03:23.411 --> 00:03:26.499
I don't know if you're into
fish or meat is your favorite,

00:03:26.499 --> 00:03:29.608
but everything tonight
is absolutely fresh.

00:03:29.608 --> 00:03:30.745
- [Gordon] Stop.

00:03:30.745 --> 00:03:32.046
- Yeah?
- Yeah.

00:03:32.046 --> 00:03:33.427
Okay.

00:03:33.427 --> 00:03:34.301
Right.

00:03:34.301 --> 00:03:37.328
What you need to do is (censored) relax.

00:03:37.328 --> 00:03:40.683
A great service is attentive
without being noticed.

00:03:40.683 --> 00:03:42.817
So far, you're making my (censored) itch,

00:03:42.817 --> 00:03:44.889
and it hasn't come out yet.

00:03:44.889 --> 00:03:46.819
That's how irritated you are.

00:03:46.819 --> 00:03:49.867
Right, ladies, sit down with
Donald and I at the table.

00:03:49.867 --> 00:03:51.492
Take an order of four.

00:03:51.492 --> 00:03:54.519
Right, off we go, here we go.

00:03:54.519 --> 00:03:56.714
- Good evening everyone.

00:03:56.714 --> 00:03:58.217
- (sighing) (censored)

00:03:58.217 --> 00:04:01.163
- And for you all, tonight,
what would you like?

00:04:01.163 --> 00:04:03.703
- [Woman] The beet root
starts and the halibut for me,

00:04:03.703 --> 00:04:04.536
please.
- The beet root

00:04:04.536 --> 00:04:06.425
and the halibut.

00:04:06.425 --> 00:04:08.823
- (censored) All right, come here, you.

00:04:08.823 --> 00:04:10.611
- Really that painful?
- Yes, it was painful.

00:04:10.611 --> 00:04:11.667
Here's the thing.

00:04:11.667 --> 00:04:12.909
Sit down.
- Yep.

00:04:12.909 --> 00:04:14.636
- Where's the pad?

00:04:14.636 --> 00:04:16.241
Look at that.

00:04:16.241 --> 00:04:19.289
I swear to god, it's the
writing of a six-year old.

00:04:19.289 --> 00:04:21.768
It's all about engaging in the customers.

00:04:21.768 --> 00:04:23.637
To start off with, darling,
what would you like to start,

00:04:23.637 --> 00:04:24.978
madame?
- Can I have prawns, please?

00:04:24.978 --> 00:04:26.603
- Prawns over Michaels?
- And the chicken.

00:04:26.603 --> 00:04:27.782
- Certainly.

00:04:27.782 --> 00:04:29.529
So, P and C.

00:04:29.529 --> 00:04:31.703
And madame?
- Mackerel Halibut please.

00:04:31.703 --> 00:04:34.304
- Mackerel Halibut,
certainly, of course, lovely.

00:04:34.304 --> 00:04:37.221
And it's just there, "P M M C C C".

00:04:38.347 --> 00:04:40.277
- That is a god damn revelation.

00:04:40.277 --> 00:04:42.878
- Relax, you don't have to
have everything in detail here.

00:04:42.878 --> 00:04:45.498
But you'll be surprised,
three tables down the line,

00:04:45.498 --> 00:04:49.135
30 tables down the line, if
you continue to work at it,

00:04:49.135 --> 00:04:52.468
you start to become incredibly charming.

00:04:53.422 --> 00:04:54.499
Do you know why?

00:04:54.499 --> 00:04:56.307
Because your confidence levels up there.

00:04:56.307 --> 00:04:58.319
Stay in front of the competition,

00:04:58.319 --> 00:05:01.102
because there's two ways in this industry.

00:05:01.102 --> 00:05:03.523
Either you move with it, or it moves you.

00:05:03.523 --> 00:05:06.205
And I've seen so many sad
stories across the decade,

00:05:06.205 --> 00:05:08.947
where chefs have just got lazy.

00:05:08.947 --> 00:05:12.279
Got lazy, given up, and lost that hunger

00:05:12.279 --> 00:05:13.722
to be competitive,

00:05:13.722 --> 00:05:15.469
because it's the best job in the world

00:05:15.469 --> 00:05:17.643
that's not really a job, it's a passion.

00:05:17.643 --> 00:05:19.492
Because when you're rubbing shoulders,

00:05:19.492 --> 00:05:22.052
and here we are now, 10 years later,

00:05:22.052 --> 00:05:24.153
and I've got a restaurant
next door to Jamie Oliver's,

00:05:24.153 --> 00:05:26.713
and we're both literally two meters apart.

00:05:26.713 --> 00:05:30.880
Phenomenal chef, and we
keep each other on our toes.

00:05:31.752 --> 00:05:33.174
So that's exciting for me,

00:05:33.174 --> 00:05:36.872
and that's been the one key
issue across the ten years

00:05:36.872 --> 00:05:39.046
of filming "Kitchen Nightmares".

00:05:39.046 --> 00:05:41.728
Never, ever, ever give up.

00:05:41.728 --> 00:05:45.385
My first passion before
food was, was football.

00:05:45.385 --> 00:05:48.087
But I was always an eater,
great lover of food,

00:05:48.087 --> 00:05:51.561
and I grew up partly in
Scotland, partly down South.

00:05:51.561 --> 00:05:53.593
So we had that sort of
token of appreciation

00:05:53.593 --> 00:05:56.153
for home-cooked food.

00:05:56.153 --> 00:05:58.794
Then I was very lucky to
get on foundation course,

00:05:58.794 --> 00:06:01.944
spending the last year of my
school at a foundation course.

00:06:01.944 --> 00:06:04.747
Studying math, english,
two and half days a week,

00:06:04.747 --> 00:06:06.413
and then learning to cook for
two and a half days a week,

00:06:06.413 --> 00:06:08.407
and it was sort of a new experiment.

00:06:08.407 --> 00:06:11.211
And then we had trouble with
buying my first set of whites,

00:06:11.211 --> 00:06:13.629
and chef knives, and we depending
on the bamboo round table

00:06:13.629 --> 00:06:17.083
to come up with something
like 85, 90 pounds

00:06:17.083 --> 00:06:18.505
my mum and dad didn't have to get me

00:06:18.505 --> 00:06:19.805
my first set of whites.

00:06:19.805 --> 00:06:23.972
So I remember that day now and
how ecstatic and happy I was

00:06:24.885 --> 00:06:27.302
when I got given my first
set of whites, my knives,

00:06:27.302 --> 00:06:29.415
and all of the sudden I
wanted to go on that mission.

00:06:29.415 --> 00:06:31.833
Football was a sort of
huge burning ambition,

00:06:31.833 --> 00:06:34.678
and I was trying to decide.

00:06:34.678 --> 00:06:37.664
Yeah, I had a horrific injury
where I smashed my cartilage

00:06:37.664 --> 00:06:40.062
and then all of the
sudden, what do you do,

00:06:40.062 --> 00:06:41.281
you sit there and get bitter,

00:06:41.281 --> 00:06:43.780
but I had this little burning
desire that this sort of,

00:06:43.780 --> 00:06:45.863
this chef, this ambition,

00:06:46.746 --> 00:06:48.859
and then I sort of
channeled that frustration

00:06:48.859 --> 00:06:50.403
from football into cooking.

00:06:50.403 --> 00:06:53.413
In the way that I just wanted to learn.

00:06:53.413 --> 00:06:55.770
The foundation course was huge.

00:06:55.770 --> 00:06:57.883
Their sitting yield's 706.

00:06:57.883 --> 00:07:00.972
I was a mature, part-time student,

00:07:00.972 --> 00:07:02.638
and mum and dad didn't have the money

00:07:02.638 --> 00:07:04.507
to send me to college full-time,

00:07:04.507 --> 00:07:06.417
so it was hard.

00:07:06.417 --> 00:07:08.530
But what I did do was take
a job in the industry,

00:07:08.530 --> 00:07:09.920
I worked six days a
week and went to college

00:07:09.920 --> 00:07:10.910
one day a week.

00:07:10.910 --> 00:07:12.413
So then everything
started becoming clearer.

00:07:12.413 --> 00:07:14.099
I got my qualifications, the basic,

00:07:14.099 --> 00:07:15.440
and then went to London.

00:07:15.440 --> 00:07:17.675
And that's where it really just took off.

00:07:17.675 --> 00:07:20.459
This is my little niçoise,
but a specialized niçoise.

00:07:20.459 --> 00:07:22.491
And it's the kind of
thing I pick myself up

00:07:22.491 --> 00:07:24.299
after cycling 200 K.

00:07:24.299 --> 00:07:27.288
A beautiful piece of tuna,
going to brush that first,

00:07:27.288 --> 00:07:29.543
just a little bit of mustard,
stops it from drying out.

00:07:29.543 --> 00:07:30.925
Now, sesame seeds.

00:07:30.925 --> 00:07:33.932
Just beautifully roll that down.

00:07:33.932 --> 00:07:36.654
I always think about food everywhere I go.

00:07:36.654 --> 00:07:39.560
So when I'm training, I'm
never very far away from food.

00:07:39.560 --> 00:07:41.978
I had that extraordinary
moment at the start of Kona,

00:07:41.978 --> 00:07:44.558
two years ago, where I was pedaling,

00:07:44.558 --> 00:07:46.023
at sort of half past five in the morning,

00:07:46.023 --> 00:07:47.974
awaiting for that
amazing cannon to go off.

00:07:47.974 --> 00:07:49.233
To get rid of the nerves,

00:07:49.233 --> 00:07:52.301
I just started thinking
about recipes and dishes

00:07:52.301 --> 00:07:55.654
and how do I get through this 3.8 K swim.

00:07:55.654 --> 00:07:59.298
Garnish, very simple, you've got potatoes.

00:07:59.298 --> 00:08:02.054
We'll start with a little
teaspoon of olive oil.

00:08:02.054 --> 00:08:04.456
And we'll get these
potatoes nice and crispy.

00:08:04.456 --> 00:08:07.951
Cut them in half, and then
we'll put that side down.

00:08:07.951 --> 00:08:11.951
So, in my bowl, got some
really nice slot rings.

00:08:13.741 --> 00:08:16.606
Olive niçoise, black olives.

00:08:16.606 --> 00:08:18.023
Some green beans.

00:08:19.105 --> 00:08:22.153
And then in there, I'm
just going to open up

00:08:22.153 --> 00:08:23.941
some of these little anchovies.

00:08:23.941 --> 00:08:25.444
A little touch of vinaigrette.

00:08:25.444 --> 00:08:27.435
This is classic vinaigrette
made with lots of lemon juice.

00:08:27.435 --> 00:08:30.605
And then look, just mix that up, nicely.

00:08:30.605 --> 00:08:31.986
With some fresh parsley.

00:08:31.986 --> 00:08:33.774
Your tomatoes.

00:08:33.774 --> 00:08:35.420
House made vinegar.

00:08:35.420 --> 00:08:37.498
Fresh lemon juice in,
that lifts everything.

00:08:37.498 --> 00:08:40.141
You work 14, 15 hours a day to perfect

00:08:40.141 --> 00:08:42.620
an absolutely stunning
dish that disappears

00:08:42.620 --> 00:08:44.103
in two and a half minutes.

00:08:44.103 --> 00:08:45.688
And that's what I said, it loses you.

00:08:45.688 --> 00:08:49.386
You get on that journey
and nothing else matters

00:08:49.386 --> 00:08:51.092
except what you put on the plate.

00:08:51.092 --> 00:08:52.901
Because you start off with
these raw ingredients,

00:08:52.901 --> 00:08:53.937
and then you're off to that journey.

00:08:53.937 --> 00:08:56.903
60 minutes later, you've
got this bit of magic.

00:08:56.903 --> 00:08:58.798
Also, it's an incredible passion.

00:08:58.798 --> 00:09:00.381
It's a huge canvas.

00:09:01.744 --> 00:09:05.911
Take your summer leaves, I
want to use the little hearts,

00:09:07.656 --> 00:09:10.379
and just open them up, gently,

00:09:10.379 --> 00:09:12.329
without sort of ripping them.

00:09:12.329 --> 00:09:16.496
Get down to the heart and
cut the hearts into four.

00:09:18.242 --> 00:09:19.867
Take this.

00:09:19.867 --> 00:09:21.838
Nice and crispy.

00:09:21.838 --> 00:09:25.312
And then from there, as
they start to cool down,

00:09:25.312 --> 00:09:28.096
just put a little touch
of vinaigrette over that

00:09:28.096 --> 00:09:30.679
and hit them with your parsley.

00:09:32.505 --> 00:09:33.642
Food was my calling, I think,

00:09:33.642 --> 00:09:35.898
because that was the way
I could sort of disappear.

00:09:35.898 --> 00:09:37.686
Disappear, travel, learn,

00:09:37.686 --> 00:09:39.311
and get really excited about something.

00:09:39.311 --> 00:09:41.261
And my first dream was to go to France

00:09:41.261 --> 00:09:44.045
to understand why were
they the sort of foundation

00:09:44.045 --> 00:09:46.747
of cooking, why did they start
it, how did they start it?

00:09:46.747 --> 00:09:48.962
And I disappeared, I became French,

00:09:48.962 --> 00:09:51.522
and within 18 months, I
was fluent in the language,

00:09:51.522 --> 00:09:53.371
I was holding my own in a foreign kitchen,

00:09:53.371 --> 00:09:57.150
and I was seriously cooking my ass off.

00:09:57.150 --> 00:10:01.294
Now, for the dressing, very, very simple.

00:10:01.294 --> 00:10:05.461
Touch of the vinaigrette, and
then some fresh lemon juice.

00:10:07.694 --> 00:10:09.076
That gives a nice lightness,

00:10:09.076 --> 00:10:11.148
vibrancy to the dish as well.

00:10:11.148 --> 00:10:13.586
And just lightly dress them.

00:10:13.586 --> 00:10:16.126
My first set of knives
was bought by a charity

00:10:16.126 --> 00:10:17.264
because my parents couldn't afford

00:10:17.264 --> 00:10:18.889
to send me to cooking school.

00:10:18.889 --> 00:10:20.799
And this knife wallet was a swimbo,

00:10:20.799 --> 00:10:22.851
it was bright yellow
handles, and trust me,

00:10:22.851 --> 00:10:25.249
I couldn't cut butter,
but they looked the part.

00:10:25.249 --> 00:10:27.240
My mother had a tear in her
eye because she was upset

00:10:27.240 --> 00:10:28.499
she couldn't afford it.

00:10:28.499 --> 00:10:30.612
My first set of whites and
my first set of knives,

00:10:30.612 --> 00:10:31.953
I've still got them.

00:10:31.953 --> 00:10:33.660
Finally, the tuna.

00:10:33.660 --> 00:10:34.920
Get the pan nice and hot.

00:10:34.920 --> 00:10:37.480
Put a teaspoon of oil in there.

00:10:37.480 --> 00:10:41.610
Tuna into the pan, 90 seconds, each side.

00:10:41.610 --> 00:10:43.209
Out.

00:10:43.209 --> 00:10:46.059
And literally squeeze some
fresh lemon juice on there.

00:10:46.059 --> 00:10:49.046
When you slice it, let
the knife do the work.

00:10:49.046 --> 00:10:51.362
Fingers on top and just slice through.

00:10:51.362 --> 00:10:53.353
Nice and carefully.

00:10:53.353 --> 00:10:54.186
